@@s001 Lazy foldRight
@@s002 ora:head just computed,no:0 > 2 false: the rest is needed,ok:found,spento:never computed
@@s003 <code>from(0).exists(_ &gt; 2)</code>. An infinite list: at the start nothing is computed, there are only "recipes" (the question marks).
@@s004 Head 0: 0 &gt; 2 is false. <code>false || rest</code> must look at the right part, so it <b>forces</b> the rest.
@@s005 Head 1: still false, forced again.
@@s006 Head 2: false.
@@s007 Head 3: 3 &gt; 2 is <b>true</b>. <code>true || something</code> is true without looking at the something: the rest is <b>never forced</b>.
@@s008 Result <b>true</b>, only the heads 0, 1, 2, 3 of an infinite list computed (checked by printing the forcings). It works because the second argument of f is by-name.
@@s009 ok:computed right away,due:recipe (by-name)
@@s010 ora:foldRight,due:foldLeft,ok:final formula
@@s011 ok:computed,spento:never computed
@@s012 ok:kept,no:first that fails: stop,spento:never looked at
@@s013 dato:this,due:that (at the bottom: it is z),ok:result
@@s014 ok:computed,spento:never computed,no:computed for nothing
@@s015 Lazy foldRight
@@s016 On ordinary lists <code>foldRight</code> always consumes everything. On a LazyList no: the function receives the rest <b>as a recipe</b>, and if it does not use it the fold stops. That is why it works also on infinite lists, and it is the basis of exercises 6–9.
@@s017 The signature of the course, piece by piece
@@s018 foldRight on LazyList
@@s019 The key point: the <b>second</b> argument of <code>f</code> is by-name (<code>=> C</code>). <code>f</code> receives "the result of the rest" without it having been computed.
@@s020 This expression passes to <code>f</code> as a recipe. It is run only if <code>f</code> uses its second parameter.
@@s021 The head instead is computed right away, because the first parameter of <code>f</code> is ordinary.
@@s022 <code>z</code> too is by-name: it is computed only if one really reaches the bottom.
@@s023 <code>C &gt;: B</code> is a help for the compiler (it is explained in the comment of the file): it allows starting from a value such as <code>None</code> or <code>empty</code> and obtaining a more general type, such as <code>Option[A]</code>.
@@s024 Head at once, rest as a recipe
@@s025 <code>foldRight</code> and <code>foldLeft</code>: where <code>z</code> ends up
@@s026 The file already has both (written by the teacher). To see them, instead of doing sums I print the formula they build.
@@s027 foldRight on (1, 2, 3)
@@s028 dato:element,due:suspended rest,ok:z
@@s029 <code>f</code> receives the head and the <b>recipe</b> of the result on the rest.
@@s030 If <code>f</code> uses the recipe, one step down.
@@s031 One more.
@@s032 Finite list: in place of the rest there is <code>z</code>. It is combined <b>from the bottom</b>. Real output: <code>f(1, f(2, f(3, z)))</code>.
@@s033 foldLeft on (1, 2, 3)
@@s034 dato:element,ora:new accumulator,ok:result
@@s035 No recipe: <code>f(h(), z)</code> becomes the new <code>z</code> and one restarts from the tail.
@@s036 The accumulator grows while it unrolls.
@@s037 The recursive call is the last thing that happens: tail recursion.
@@s038 Real output. Same elements, brackets reversed: <code>1</code> is the first to meet <code>z</code>.
@@s039 Test (verified)
@@s040 foldRight
@@s041 foldLeft
@@s042 rebuild with <code>(a, acc) => cons(a, acc)</code>
@@s043 <code>List(1, 2, 3)</code>
@@s044 <code>List(3, 2, 1)</code>: reversed
@@s045 on an infinite list
@@s046 it stops if <code>f</code> does not use <code>acc</code>: <code>naturals.foldRight(false)((a, acc) => a &gt; 3 || acc)</code> → <code>true</code>
@@s047 never terminates: it always reaches the bottom
@@s048 Practical rule
@@s049 On <code>LazyList</code> one starts from <code>foldRight</code>. The comment in the file on <code>foldLeft</code> says so: <i>"Eager; cannot be used to work with infinite lazy lists"</i> and <i>"Even if f does not force z, foldLeft will continue to recurse"</i>. <code>foldLeft</code> is useful on finite lists, when you want constant stack or precisely the reversed order.
@@s050 The formula built by each fold
@@s051 <code>exists</code> that stops
@@s052 from(0).exists(_ &gt; 2)
@@s053 0 &gt; 2 is false: <code>||</code> must look at the right part, so it forces the rest.
@@s054 Same for 1.
@@s055 And for 2.
@@s056 3 &gt; 2: <code>true ||</code> something is true without looking at the something. The rest is <b>never forced</b>.
@@s057 Only the heads 0, 1, 2, 3 computed (checked by printing the forcings), on an infinite list.
@@s058 Only what is needed is computed
@@s059 <code>headOption</code> with foldRight (exercise 7)
@@s060 from(0).headOption1
@@s061 <code>f</code> ignores the rest (<code>_</code>): it does not use it, so it is not computed.
@@s062 Only one head computed (checked). On Empty the result is <code>z = None</code>.
@@s063 Doubt: then does the foldRight serve no purpose here?
@@s064 It is precisely the point of the exercise: to show that foldRight <b>can</b> stop at the first step. On an ordinary List the same code would traverse the whole list before answering.
@@s065 Only one head computed
@@s066 <code>takeWhile</code> with foldRight (exercise 6)
@@s067 Your solution
@@s068 If the list is finite, the prefix is empty.
@@s069 <code>a</code> is the element, <code>acc</code> is (the recipe of) the takeWhile of the rest.
@@s070 If <code>a</code> is fine, I keep it and attach the rest to it. <code>cons</code> is by-name: <code>acc</code> is <b>not</b> forced here.
@@s071 At the first element that is not fine, I stop: <code>empty</code> without looking at <code>acc</code>.
@@s072 Stopping at the first that is not fine
@@s073 map, filter, append, flatMap (exercise 8)
@@s074 All with <code>foldRight</code>, without explicit recursion. The trick is always the same: build with <code>cons</code>, so that the rest (<code>acc</code>) stays a recipe.
@@s075 The four functions
@@s076 <b>Signatures</b>: the file deliberately leaves them wrong (<code>Any</code>), they must be designed. They are copied from those of the lists.
@@s077 <b>map</b>: each element transformed, in front of the transformed rest. <code>empty[B]</code> tells the compiler what type the empty list is.
@@s078 <b>filter</b>: I keep or skip. Attention: if <code>p</code> is never true, <code>filter</code> on an infinite list never stops.
@@s079 <b>append</b>: the text asks it to be <b>non strict</b> in the argument, so <code>that</code> is by-name. <code>B &gt;: A</code>: I can attach a list of a more general type (Int + Double → numbers).
@@s080 The starting value is <code>that</code>: at the bottom of the first list there is the second. Thanks to the by-name of <code>z</code>, <code>that</code> is computed only if one really reaches the bottom.
@@s081 <b>flatMap</b>: each element becomes a lazy list, and I attach them one after the other.
@@s082 Doubt: is A this? And why [B]?
@@s083 No, they are different things. <code>A</code> is a <b>type</b>: the type of the elements, born in <code>enum LazyList[+A]</code> and valid for all methods. <code>this</code> is a <b>value</b>: the list on which you call the method, of type <code>LazyList[A]</code>. On <code>LazyList(1, 2, 3).map(…)</code>: <code>this</code> = the list (1, 2, 3), <code>A</code> = <code>Int</code>.
@@s084 <code>B</code> instead does not exist until you declare it in square brackets after the name: <code>def map[B](…)</code>. It is chosen by the <code>f</code> at each call: <code>map(_ * 2)</code> → <code>B = Int</code>, <code>map(_.toString)</code> → <code>B = String</code>.
@@s085 Doubt: z is the starting value? Then in append does that go first?
@@s086 With <code>foldRight</code> <code>z</code> ends <b>at the bottom</b>: it is what is there when the list ends. So in <code>append</code> <code>that</code> goes <b>after</b> <code>this</code>:
@@s087 For <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code> and <code>flatMap</code> "what is there when the list ends" is <code>empty</code>; only for <code>append</code> is it <code>that</code>.
@@s088 
