@@s092 Test case of the text
@@s093 Result (checked)
@@s094 <code>naturals.map(_*2).drop(30).take(5).toList</code>
@@s095 <code>List(62, 64, 66, 68, 70)</code>
@@s096 <code>naturals.drop(42).filter(_%2 == 0).take(5).toList</code>
@@s097 <code>List(44, 46, 48, 50, 52)</code>
@@s098 <code>naturals.take(3).append(naturals).take(6).toList</code>
@@s099 <code>List(1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3)</code>
@@s100 <code>naturals.flatMap(to).take(10).toList</code>
@@s101 <code>List(1, 0, -1, …, -8)</code>
@@s102 Why flatMap(to) never reaches 2
@@s103 <code>to(1)</code> is infinite: 1, 0, −1, … . <code>flatMap</code> attaches <code>to(2)</code> <b>after</b> the end of <code>to(1)</code>, which never arrives. With <code>take(10)</code> you get only pieces of <code>to(1)</code>.
@@s104 Measured detail: <code>from(0).map(_ * 2)</code>, even without <code>toList</code>, already computes the first head, because <code>foldRight</code> runs <code>h()</code> at once. No tail is touched.
@@s105 What goes in z: empty, or that
@@s106 Exercise 9: why <code>find</code> with <code>filter</code> is fine here
@@s107 On LazyList
@@s108 <code>from(0).find(_ &gt; 2)</code>: <code>filter</code> builds the result lazily, <code>headOption</code> asks only for the first. Computed 0, 1, 2, 3 and that is all (checked).
@@s109 On List
@@s110 <code>List(1, 2, 3, 4).filter(_ &gt; 2).headOption</code>: <code>filter</code> is strict, it checks <b>all</b> 4 elements and builds a whole list, then one of them is used (checked by printing).
@@s111 Answer in English (the exam may have open questions)
@@s112 "On a lazy list, filter does not traverse the whole list: it only produces elements when they are demanded. headOption demands just the first one, so find stops as soon as it meets the first element satisfying p. On a strict List, filter would first test every element and build a new list, only to keep its head, which wastes time and memory, and would not terminate on an infinite structure."
@@s113 Doubt: why Some? Can I write Some(x > 2)?
@@s114 <code>find</code> may find nothing, so it returns an <code>Option</code>: <code>LazyList(1, 2, 3).find(_ &gt; 10)</code> → <code>None</code>. Inside <code>Some</code> there is <b>the element found</b>, not the condition: <code>find(_ &gt; 2)</code> → <code>Some(3)</code>. <code>Some(x &gt; 2)</code> would be <code>Some(true)</code>: an <code>Option[Boolean]</code>.
@@s115 It is the <b>first</b> element that satisfies <code>p</code>: <code>LazyList(1, 2, 3, 4).find(_ &gt; 2)</code> → <code>Some(3)</code>, the 4 is not even looked at. If you only need to know whether one exists, there is <code>exists</code>, which gives <code>true</code>/<code>false</code>.
@@s116 filter + headOption: lazy against strict
